# Portal Genie Admin — Project Standards

Internal Angular admin app for The Portal Genie. Official product name: **The Portal Genie**. App name: **Portal Genie Admin**.

Before implementing, read `README.md`, `docs/BRAND_AND_DESIGN.md`, and the relevant file in `docs/features/`.

## Scope

- This repository is **frontend only**.
- Do not implement backends, databases, auth servers, Postmark, or email sending.
- Where a backend will be needed, define TypeScript interfaces and service abstractions. Use mock/fixture adapters behind those interfaces until APIs exist.
- Do not hard-code data that will come from APIs. Seed data belongs in mock adapters, not in components.

## Quality

- Production-quality TypeScript. Prefer explicit types; avoid `any`.
- Small, focused, reusable components. Separate presentation, business logic, and data access.
- No unnecessary dependencies. Do not add libraries when Angular already covers the need.
- Accessible, responsive, and keyboard-usable UI. Every list/detail view needs loading, empty, and error states.
- Forms use Angular reactive forms with visible validation. Never rely on placeholder text as the only label.

## Structure and naming

Target a feature-based Angular layout once the app is generated:

- `core/` — singleton services, interceptors, configuration
- `shared/` — reusable UI and utilities with no feature coupling
- `features/<name>/` — routes, pages, feature components, feature services
- `layout/` — shell, navigation, page frame

Files: kebab-case. Classes: PascalCase. Services end with `Service`. Models/interfaces live in `*.model.ts` or `*.types.ts`.

## Modules

- **Customer Engagement** is the first feature. Follow `docs/features/customer-engagement.md`.
- **Customer Usage** is documented only. Do not design or implement it until requested.
- Keep features modular. Share customer metric types via contracts, not by coupling UIs.
